From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] gpu-broker: a client's presents go over a connection of their own (WINE_PS5_PRESENT_LINK=1)

A client's connection to the broker carries one call at a time, under one lock in the client. With the asynchronous
present (0024), the WSI's presenter thread presents while the game's queue thread submits, but both called on that one
connection: while the display copied a frame (6.4 ms) and waited for a free buffer, the submissions waited behind the
present. A Direct3D 12 game at 119.88 Hz spent 8.05 ms a frame in submission round trips that way (PS5_Proton
2026-10-17, record removed from the tree; in git history), against 2.80 ms without the asynchronous present (2026-10-17).

With WINE_PS5_PRESENT_LINK=1 the client asks at its first present for a present link (PW_GPU_PRESENT_LINK, protocol.h):
the broker makes a socket pair, serves its end on a second thread for the same client, and sends the other end back;
from then on SWAPCHAIN, WINDOW_SWAPCHAIN and TIMING go over it under a lock of their own, everything else as before. The
link takes those three alone (anything else: EINVAL). The broker reads a present's frame outside its lock, so the
frame is pinned there: a FREE of a buffer it overlaps waits for that present (pin_released). Closing a client joins
both threads and closes both sockets. Refused or not asked for, every call goes over the one connection as before. One
line each side: "gpu-wine cookie= pid= present_link= status=" in the broker's record, "gpu-client present link=
status=" in the client's. Off by default.
Console: a Direct3D 12 game at 119.88 Hz with the asynchronous present, the owner off the pad, the same settings and
stretch (PS5_Proton, 2026-10-18, one run without it and one with it; record removed from the tree; in git history): submission round trips 8.06 to 0.29 ms a frame, 1%
low 87.9-71.4 to 100.0-97.6 fps, frames over 20 ms 20 to 3, 119.0 to 119.8 fps; the title 1.06 to 1.35 CPU-s/s.
